"An attack graph connects security conditions and assets through feasible steps an adversary could chain together.\n\nUse when: Review multi-step exposure paths toward a critical asset. Identify shared choke points across several modeled attack paths.\n\nAvoid when: A single goal decomposition is sufficient without shared intermediate conditions.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote assets, privileges, or explicitly defined security conditions. Directed edges identify exploit or access transitions with preconditions. Evidence markers distinguish observed exposure from assumed or simulated feasibility.\n\nBuild: Define the attacker’s initial access and target conditions. Collect asset, identity, and exposure evidence with capture time. Connect only transitions whose prerequisites are satisfied or clearly assumed. Lay out stages toward the target and route converging paths through separate ports.\n\nCheck: Every transition states the condition enabling the next step. Reachability is not reported as confirmed compromise. Shared prerequisite logic is explicit when several conditions are required. Path highlights and evidence notes do not obscure asset names.\n\nPitfalls: Treating an automated path as proof an attacker traversed it. Inferring exploitability from network connectivity alone.\n\nExample: An exposed workload can access an overprivileged role, which can read a sensitive storage resource under stated assumptions.\n\nAlternatives: Attack tree, Authorization graph."
